  "function_translated_nl": "Lesson context: lesson_05. Let triangle PQR be a triangle with sides PQ = 30, QR = 50. Let segment XY be a segment. Let segment PX be a segment with length 12. Let segment PY be a segment with length 9. Let segment QR be a segment with length 50. Let point X be a point on PQ. Let point Y be a point on PR. By the triangle proportionality theorem, a line parallel to QR divides the other two sides proportionally into segments PX and PY. Find the length of XY.",
  "student_facing_prose": "A surveyor is mapping out a triangular plot of land PQR, where side PQ measures 30 meters and side QR measures 50 meters. She marks point X on side PQ, 12 meters from P, and point Y on side PR, 9 meters from P. She then draws segment XY to connect these two points.\n\nBy the triangle proportionality theorem, when a line segment divides two sides of a triangle proportionally, that segment must be parallel to the third side. Since X and Y divide sides PQ and PR in the same ratio (PX = 12 and PY = 9 create the same proportional relationship), segment XY is parallel to QR.\n\nBecause XY is parallel to QR, triangle PXY is a dilation of triangle PQR, centered at vertex P. This means the sides of the two triangles are proportional.\n\nWhat is the length of XY?"
}